# shop on plain Docker Compose

Exported from Levelrail. Nothing here needs Levelrail to run.

## Start

1. `cp .env.example .env`
2. Replace every `CHANGE_ME` in `.env`. Secrets are never exported; re-enter them or restore them from your own backup.
3. `docker compose up -d`

## Data

Named volumes start empty. To restore a volume from a tar archive of its contents (for example one made with a Levelrail backup download):

```
docker compose create
docker run --rm -v <project>_<volume>:/data -v "$PWD":/backup alpine tar xzf /backup/<volume>.tar.gz -C /data
```

- volume shop_db (db, mounted at /var/lib/postgresql/data)
- volume shop_uploads (web, mounted at /uploads)

## Databases

The database services keep the same engine and version tag as before. Dump on the old host, restore on the new one:

```
# db (postgres:16.4)
docker compose exec -T db pg_dump -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB" > db.sql
docker compose exec -T db psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" "$POSTGRES_DB" < db.sql
```

## Domains and TLS

- web: shop.example.com (port 3000)

Certificates are not exported. Point these names at your host and put any reverse proxy with automatic TLS in front of the published port. The `x-domains` field in the compose file is ignored by Docker.

## Not exported

Secret values, backup data, TLS certificates, and Levelrail features such as previews, auto-sleep and deploy hooks. Private registry logins must be redone with `docker login`.
